<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medusa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gorgo, the Gorgon, Médousa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Medusa was one of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgons</a>, usually described as daughters of Phorcys and Ceto, and the only one of them who was mortal. She is generally described as a woman with living snakes for hair, whose appearance turned anyone who looked upon her to stone. The hero Perseus beheaded her while looking at her reflection in a mirrored shield from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and the winged horse Pegasus and the giant Chrysaor sprang from her body. Perseus used the head as a weapon before giving it to Athena for her shield. In a late version by the Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, she was a beautiful maiden whom the goddess punished by turning her hair into snakes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medusa.jpg" alt="Medusa" width="330" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caravaggio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medusa_by_Carvaggio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> and Apollodorus describe the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgons</a>, Medusa, Stheno and Euryale, as offspring of the sea-god Phorcys and his sister Ceto, while Hyginus gives their parents as Gorgon and Ceto. According to Hesiod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, Medusa lived and died on Sarpedon, near Cisthene. The 2nd-century BCE writer Dionysius Scytobrachion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> place the Gorgons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. The scholar Jane Ellen Harrison argued that the Gorgoneion was originally a ritual mask misunderstood, and that the tripling of Medusa into three sisters was secondary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In most versions King Polydectes of Seriphus sent Perseus to fetch Medusa's head because he wanted to marry Perseus's mother. The gods helped him with a mirrored shield from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, winged sandals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, a sword from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>'s helm of invisibility. Perseus killed her while looking at her reflection. She was pregnant by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, and Pegasus and the giant Chrysaor sprang from her body. The severed head kept its power to turn onlookers to stone, and Perseus used it as a weapon before giving it to Athena, who set it on her shield, the Aegis.</p><p>In antiquity the head appeared as the Gorgoneion, a device to avert evil. From the 5th century BCE artists also showed Medusa as beautiful, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> speaks of fair-cheeked Medusa in an ode of 490 BCE. In the late version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> she was a beautiful maiden whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> punished for mating with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> in her temple by turning her hair into snakes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Joseph Campbell read the myth as the memory of a historical invasion, in which Hellenes overran a goddess's shrines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, in an essay published in 1940, took the beheading to stand for castration. In the 20th century feminists reassessed Medusa, and many adopted her face as a symbol of female rage; Hélène Cixous argued in The Laugh of the Medusa that men's retelling turned her into a monster. Artists have returned to her often, from a vase by Polygnotos of about 450 to 440 BCE to Benvenuto Cellini's bronze Perseus (1554), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>'s Medusa (1597) and Antonio Canova's marble Perseus with the Head of Medusa (1801). Her head appears on the flag and emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Medusa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Painters turned to drama and landscape: Friedrich’s Wanderer above the Sea of Fog of about 1818, Théodore Géricault’s The Raft of the <b>Medusa</b> of 1819, the skies of J. M. W. Turner and John Constable.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
